module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceBlowupPresentation
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceQuasiCoherentIdeal
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceModuleOperations
public import WeilConjectures.Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.RegularPrincipal

import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceArtinRees
import Sheaf.TraceFormula.EtaleGradedModuleAlgebra
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceMorphismProperties
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceFibreProductsEtaleCovers
import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.ClosedImmersion

/-! # The universal lifting property of an algebraic-space blowup

Source: Stacks Project, Spaces Divisors, Tag 085U, lemma-universal-property-blowing-up
(blueprint: lem-sfb-space-blowup-universal-property).

The Cartier hypothesis is written on affine open neighbourhoods in each etale scheme
chart. The ideal is the image of the canonical tensor map, transported along the
existing regular-function comparison. Regular principal means generated by one
non-zero-divisor; no condition on the source or target's separation is imposed.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false

universe u

open CategoryTheory Opposite

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace

/-- A map whose extended center is an effective Cartier divisor lifts uniquely to the
specified blowup presentation. The chartwise regular-principal condition states that
on each etale scheme chart every point has an affine open neighbourhood where the
image ideal is generated by a non-zero-divisor. It uses the canonical `idealTensorMap`,
not an arbitrary map from the pulled-back module to the structure sheaf.
Source: Stacks Project, Spaces Divisors, Tag 085U
(blueprint: lem-sfb-space-blowup-universal-property).

Proof:
1. Put J = im(f^* K -> O_Y). The ideal-power definitions and affine-chart calculus
   (def-sfb-space-ideal-power-products, lem-sfb-space-qcoh-affine-chart-calculus)
   identify K^n with the image of tensor multiplication. The Rees algebra is generated
   in degree one. Locally J = (t) with t regular, so J^{tensor n} -> J^n is an
   isomorphism, including n = 0; these multiplication identifications glue.
2. The canonical tensor map (def-sfb-space-quasi-coherent-ideal and
   def-trf-space-etale-module-functors) induces maps f^*(K^n) -> J^n by multiplying
   local sections. They factor uniquely through J^n and respect products and units
   after inclusion into O_Y. The degree-one map is surjective by the sheaf-image
   definition. This gives the graded map of def-trf-etale-graded-module-algebra.
3. Apply lem-sfb-space-proj-degree-one-quotients to this line quotient. The blowup
   presentation identifies its Rees Proj with B, giving a lift over X.
4. For two lifts, lem-sfb-space-blowup-chart-properties gives agreement away from
   V(J) and separatedness of B -> X. By def-sfb-space-morphism-properties,
   def-weil-closed-immersion and lem-trf-space-fibre-products-etale-covers their
   equalizer is a closed subspace containing that complement. On an affine chart
   with J = (t), its ideal H becomes zero after inverting t. Thus each a in H has
   t^n a = 0 for some n; regularity gives a = 0. The equalizer is all of Y, hence
   the lifts agree. This is the explicit local argument of Tags 083S and 084N. -/
theorem existsUnique_lift_blowupPresentation
    {S : Scheme.{u}} {X Y : AlgebraicSpace S}
    (K : QuasiCoherentIdeal X) (hK : (K.val : X.Modules).IsFiniteType)
    (B : BlowupPresentation K) (f : Y ⟶ X)
    (hCartier : ∀ (V : Y.EtaleSite) (y : Y.etaleForget.obj V),
      ∃ W : (Y.etaleForget.obj V).affineOpens, y ∈ W.1 ∧
        ((idealSectionImage (idealTensorMap f K) ((Y.schemeOpens V).obj W.1)).comap
          ((Y.schemeOpensRingHom V).app (op W.1)).hom :
          Ideal Γ(Y.etaleForget.obj V, W.1)).IsRegularPrincipal) :
    ∃! g : Y ⟶ B.space, g ≫ B.projection = f := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace
